        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked and Tested
          </h2>

          <p>
            We looked at the resurfacing and thin-patching products homeowners can actually buy: bagged and pailed materials from Quikrete, Sakrete, CTS Rapid Set and Henry that are stocked at big-box stores and sold on Amazon. We left out professional overlay systems that need a trained crew, a spray rig or proprietary primers, because they are not what someone resurfacing a driveway on a Saturday is choosing between.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every product was judged against its published data: application thickness range, working time, time to foot and vehicle traffic, and whether it is rated for exterior use. Those four numbers decide which product fits a job far more than brand does. A product that sets in 15 minutes is a gift on a small step repair and a disaster on a two-car driveway.
          </p>

          <p>
            We applied the main resurfacers to sections of a weathered patio slab with light scaling and pitting after pressure washing it, and used the patching products on chipped step edges and popouts. We paid attention to how forgiving each one was to mix, how long it stayed workable in warm weather, how well it took a broom finish, and whether it showed crazing or hollow-sounding spots after curing.
          </p>

          <p>
            We weighted forgiveness heavily. Resurfacing is a one-shot job: once the material starts to set, you cannot rework it, and a product with a sensible working window is worth more to a first-timer than one that is marginally stronger. We also favored manufacturers that publish clear instructions on preparation, since most resurfacing failures trace back to the surface underneath rather than the bag.
          </p>

          <p>
            Prices vary by region and package size, so we describe cost in broad tiers rather than quoting figures that will be out of date within months.
          </p>
