        <div id="can-resurface" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Can Your Slab Be Resurfaced?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Resurfacing is a surface repair, and the most important decision is whether your problem is actually on the surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            Good candidates are slabs with scaling, where the top layer has flaked off in patches after years of freeze-thaw and deicing salt; light spalling and popouts; surface pitting; worn, exposed aggregate; and staining you want to cover. If the concrete underneath is hard and sound, and the slab is level and not rocking, a resurfacer bonds well and can transform its appearance. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-prevent-concrete-spalling-in-winter/">preventing concrete spalling in winter</a> explains why this damage happens and how to stop it coming back.
          </p>

          <p>
            Poor candidates are slabs with structural problems. Sunken or heaved sections, cracks where one side sits higher than the other, wide cracks that are still growing, and concrete that crumbles when you scrape it with a screwdriver all point to a failure below the surface. A resurfacer is typically about an eighth of an inch of cement. It cannot bridge a moving crack or support a slab over a void, and the new surface will crack along the old lines within a season or two.
          </p>

          <p>
            Try a simple sounding test before you buy. Drag a length of chain or tap a steel bar across the slab and listen. Solid concrete rings; delaminated areas sound hollow and dull. Hollow spots must be chipped out and patched before resurfacing, or the new layer will pop off with the loose concrete beneath it.
          </p>

          <p>
            If more than about a third of the slab is hollow, heaved or badly cracked, resurfacing is money spent on a surface that is about to fail anyway. Compare the numbers in our <a href="/driveway-resurfacing-cost/">driveway resurfacing cost guide</a> against a replacement quote before committing.
          </p>
        </div>

