        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Resurfacer vs Patcher vs Rapid-Set Repair Mortar
          </h2>

          <p>
            The products in this category sit side by side on the shelf but do very different jobs, and buying the wrong one is the most common way to waste a weekend.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Thickness</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Use It For</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Examples</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Polymer-modified resurfacer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Feather edge to about 1/2 inch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Renewing a whole slab surface</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Quikrete Re-Cap, Sakrete Flo-Coat</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Rapid-setting resurfacer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Skim coat to 1/8 inch, more in small areas</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fast return to service</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rapid Set NewCrete</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Vinyl or polymer patcher</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Feather edge to about 1/2 inch per layer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Pits, spalls and chipped edges</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Quikrete Vinyl Patcher, Sakrete Top 'N Bond</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Rapid-set repair mortar</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Deep and formed repairs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Broken steps, structural patches</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rapid Set Cement All</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Interior skim coat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Feather edge to several inches</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Indoor floors under coverings</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Henry 549 FeatherFinish</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            A resurfacer is designed to be spread thin over a large area. It combines fine sand, portland cement and polymer modifiers that let it bond to old concrete without a separate bonding agent and cure without cracking at an eighth of an inch. It is not designed to fill deep holes; built up too thick in one pass, it can shrink, crack and lose its bond.
          </p>

          <p>
            A patcher is designed for small areas at varying depth. Vinyl and polymer-modified patchers are sticky enough to hold on a chipped step edge and can be troweled to a feather edge, which makes them the right tool for prefilling pits before a resurfacer goes on. They trowel too stiffly to cover a whole slab evenly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rapid-set repair mortars such as Cement All are much closer to high-strength concrete. They set in minutes, reach high compressive strength and can be formed and built up thick, which is why they suit rebuilding a broken step nose or setting a post. That speed makes them impractical to spread over large areas.
          </p>

          <p>
            Interior skim coats and underlayments, like Henry FeatherFinish, are a separate category. They are excellent at flattening a basement or garage floor before tile or vinyl plank goes down, and they are not made for exterior slabs that see rain, freeze-thaw and deicing salt.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Surface Prep Decides Whether It Lasts
          </h2>

          <p>
            Resurfacer bonds to the top of the old concrete. If the top of the old concrete is dirt, sealer, oil or loose cement paste, the resurfacer bonds to that instead, and comes off with it. Nearly every peeling resurfacer we have seen failed at the prep stage, not because of the product.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start by removing anything that is not sound concrete. Chip out loose and hollow-sounding areas with a hammer and cold chisel. Degrease oil stains, and remove any existing sealer, paint or coating; a slab that beads water almost certainly carries a sealer, and most resurfacers will not bond to it. Grinding or chemical stripping is the only real fix. Our guide to <a href="/sealing-old-concrete/">sealing old concrete</a> explains how to tell whether a weathered slab still carries a coating.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then clean aggressively. A garden hose and a push broom will not remove the chalky surface paste and embedded grime that stop resurfacer bonding. Use a pressure washer, ideally a gas unit around 3,000 psi with a surface cleaner or a 15 degree tip, and work methodically across the slab. Our walkthrough on <a href="/pressure-wash-driveway-before-sealing/">pressure washing a driveway</a> covers nozzle choice and distance so you clean the surface without gouging it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Patch before you resurface. Fill pits, popouts and spalled areas deeper than the resurfacer's thickness range with a patcher or repair mortar and let them cure first. Treat cracks separately, as covered below. Then, immediately before application, dampen the slab so it is saturated but has no standing water. A dry slab pulls water out of the resurfacer and weakens the bond; puddles thin the mix. Sweep or blow out any water sitting in low spots.
          </p>

          <p>
            Budget more time for prep than for application. On a typical two-car driveway, cleaning, chipping and patching can easily take a full day before the first bag is opened.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Mixing and Applying Without Lap Marks
          </h2>

          <p>
            Resurfacer is applied thin and fast, and the working window is the constraint that shapes the whole job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Work in sections. Divide the slab along its existing control joints and treat each panel as a separate pour. Mix only as much as you can spread in the working time, which for most resurfacers is roughly 20 to 30 minutes in mild weather and less in heat. A paddle mixer on a heavy half-inch drill gives a consistent, lump-free mix; hand-mixing a 40 pound bag rarely does, and the lumps show in the finish.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pour the mix onto the damp slab and spread it with a long-handled squeegee, pushing it into pores and pits. At an eighth of an inch or less, one pass usually covers well. For a thicker build, a second coat can follow once the first has set enough to walk on, following the manufacturer's timing. Keep a wet edge and do not keep working areas that have started to stiffen; overworking draws water to the surface and weakens the top layer.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">RE-CAP BY THE NUMBERS</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1/16-1/2 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Rated thickness range</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~40 sq ft</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per 40 lb bag at 1/8 inch</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">8 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Until foot traffic</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">24 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Until vehicle traffic</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Finish while the surface is still plastic. A broom drawn across the slab in one direction gives a slip-resistant texture that also hides small variations in thickness. On walkways and driveways, a broom finish is safer when wet than a smooth trowel finish, and it is much more forgiving of amateur technique.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bring a helper. One person mixing while another spreads is the difference between a seamless surface and one with visible lap lines where a batch started to set before the next one arrived.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cracks-joints" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Cracks and Control Joints
          </h2>

          <p>
            A resurfacer is a rigid layer of cement, which means it faithfully reproduces any movement underneath it. That makes cracks and joints the most important detail of the entire job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every control joint in the original slab must survive into the new surface. Those joints exist so the slab cracks where it is supposed to. Bury them and the slab cracks anyway, usually in a jagged line right through your new surface. The usual approaches are to press weatherstripping or a thin strip into each joint before resurfacing and pull it out once the material has set, or to re-cut the joint with a grooving tool or saw afterward.
          </p>

          <p>
            Existing cracks need different treatment depending on whether they move. Stable hairline cracks can be filled with a patcher and resurfaced over, though they may still show as a faint line. Moving cracks cannot be resurfaced over at all; they will reappear within a season. The right approach is to treat them like joints: seal them with a flexible polyurethane and keep them open through the new surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our guide to the <a href="/best-concrete-crack-filler/">best concrete crack filler</a> covers which sealants suit each crack type and how to set the depth with backer rod. Do the crack work before you resurface, then re-seal those lines after the resurfacer cures so the new surface and the sealant finish flush.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the slab has so many cracks that preserving them all would leave a patchwork, that is usually a sign of structural problems below the surface, and resurfacing is the wrong investment.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="weather-curing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Temperature, Weather and Curing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cement-based products cure through a chemical reaction with water, and that reaction is sensitive to temperature.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most cement resurfacers are meant to go down when air and slab temperatures are at least 50 degrees and will stay above freezing for the next day or two. Cold slows curing, so the material stays weak for longer and is vulnerable to frost. Heat and wind do the opposite: the surface dries before the cement has fully hydrated, leaving a weak, dusty top that scales early. In summer, resurface in the early morning or evening and avoid direct midday sun on dark concrete.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch the forecast for rain. A hard shower on fresh resurfacer can wash out and pit the surface. Most products need several hours to set before light rain is harmless, and longer before heavy rain is. In hot, dry weather, lightly misting the set surface a few times a day helps the cement reach full strength rather than drying out prematurely.
          </p>

          <p>
            Respect traffic times. Quikrete lists walking on Re-Cap after about eight hours and driving on it after about 24 in normal conditions, while CTS says Rapid Set NewCrete takes foot traffic in as little as two to three hours. Cold weather stretches all of those figures, sometimes considerably.
          </p>

          <p>
            Timing matters for winter, too. A resurfacer laid in late fall gets little time to cure before the first freeze and the first round of salting, and deicing salt on young cement is one of the fastest routes back to scaling. Avoid rock salt on a surface in its first year, and if you must deice, choose one of the gentler products in our <a href="/best-ice-melt-for-concrete/">ice melt for concrete</a> guide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sealing-after" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Sealing and Long-Term Care
          </h2>

          <p>
            A resurfaced slab is a new cement surface, and it benefits from the same protection new concrete does.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sealing is not mandatory, but on exterior slabs in freeze-thaw climates it is worth doing. A penetrating silane or siloxane sealer reduces how much water and salt brine the new surface absorbs, which is exactly the mechanism that caused the scaling you just covered. Wait for the resurfacer to cure fully before sealing. Waiting periods vary from a few days to around a month depending on the product and the sealer, so check both labels.
          </p>

          <p>
            For driveways, see our <a href="/best-concrete-driveway-sealer-reviews/">concrete driveway sealer reviews</a> for products that handle tire traffic and road salt. For patios and walks, our <a href="/best-concrete-sealer/">best concrete sealer</a> guide explains the choice between penetrating sealers, which leave the surface looking natural, and film-forming acrylics, which add sheen but need recoating more often.
          </p>

          <p>
            Maintenance after that is simple. Keep the joint sealant intact and re-seal it when it pulls away, avoid piling salty snow on the slab, and clean oil stains promptly so they do not soak in. Reapply a penetrating sealer every few years, or whenever water stops beading on the surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            Set realistic expectations. A well-prepared resurfacer on a sound slab can last many years, but it is still a thin layer. Heavy trucks, snowplow blades and steel-wheeled equipment will wear it faster than the original concrete. Treat it as a new wearing surface that needs looking after, not a permanent coat of armor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Resurfacing vs Replacement: The Math
          </h2>

          <p>
            The appeal of resurfacing is cost. A DIY resurfacing job on a driveway costs a small fraction of tearing out and repouring, and it avoids demolition, disposal and days without the driveway.
          </p>

          <p>
            Material cost is modest. At an eighth of an inch, a 40 pound bag of Re-Cap covers roughly 40 square feet, so a typical single-car driveway needs a stack of bags rather than a truckload of concrete. Add a pressure washer rental, a paddle mixer, a squeegee and some patching material, and most DIY projects stay firmly in the budget tier compared with any replacement quote.
          </p>

          <p>
            Professional resurfacing, often with spray-applied or stamped decorative overlays, costs more but usually still undercuts replacement. Our <a href="/driveway-resurfacing-cost/">driveway resurfacing cost guide</a> breaks down typical per-square-foot pricing for concrete and asphalt, and our <a href="/driveway-sealer-vs-resurfacer/">sealer vs resurfacer comparison</a> helps if you are not sure the slab needs more than a coat of sealer.
          </p>

          <p>
            The math flips when the slab is structurally failing. Resurfacing a heaved, badly cracked driveway means paying twice: once now and again for replacement a few years later. If the sounding test turns up widespread hollow areas, or sections have settled noticeably, get a replacement quote before buying any bags.
          </p>

          <p>
            The middle ground is often the best value. Patch and resurface the sound panels, seal every crack and joint properly, and set aside a budget for the one or two sections that are genuinely failing. That approach buys years of good appearance without pretending a surface coat can fix a problem underneath.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Resurfacing over a sealed or dirty slab</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Resurfacer bonds to whatever is on top of the concrete. Over a sealer, oil film or grime, it bonds to that layer and peels off with it, often within weeks. If water beads on the slab, strip or grind it first, then pressure wash thoroughly before a single bag is opened.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Burying the control joints</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Joints exist so the slab cracks along a planned line. Cover them with resurfacer and the slab cracks anyway, in a random line through your new surface. Mark every joint before you start, and either keep it open with a strip during application or re-cut it once the resurfacer has set.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying it too thick</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Resurfacers are built for thin coats, generally no more than about half an inch and ideally an eighth. Poured thick to fill dips, they can shrink, crack and debond. Fill low spots and deep spalls first with a patcher or repair mortar, let them cure, then resurface at the recommended thickness.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Spreading on a dry or puddled slab</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A dry slab sucks water out of the mix and weakens the bond; standing water thins it. The target is saturated surface dry: wet enough that the concrete has absorbed all it can, with no puddles on top. Dampen the slab just before spreading and blow out any water sitting in low spots.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing too much at once</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Resurfacers give you roughly 20 to 30 minutes of working time, and less in heat. A batch that stiffens before it is spread leaves lap marks and weak spots. Mix partial batches sized to one section, have a helper keep mixing while you spread, and never add water to a mix that has started to set.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best concrete resurfacer for a driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most DIY driveway jobs, Quikrete Re-Cap is the best all-round choice. It spreads from a feather edge to half an inch, bonds to properly cleaned and dampened concrete without a separate primer, and takes vehicle traffic after about 24 hours. Sakrete Flo-Coat is a close alternative, and Rapid Set NewCrete suits jobs that must be back in service within hours.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does concrete resurfacer last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On a sound, well-prepared slab with its joints preserved and a penetrating sealer on top, a resurfacer can last many years. The biggest variables are preparation and climate. Resurfacer laid over dirty concrete, over moving cracks, or in late fall just before a hard winter can fail in a single season. Heavy vehicles and plow blades also shorten its life.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you resurface concrete that has cracks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if the cracks are stable and treated first. Hairline static cracks can be patched and resurfaced over, though they may still show faintly. Moving cracks come straight back through a rigid resurfacer, so seal them with flexible polyurethane and keep them open like joints. Wide or displaced cracks point to a structural problem that resurfacing will not fix.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How thick should concrete resurfacer be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most resurfacers perform best at about an eighth of an inch, and the common products are rated from a feather edge up to around half an inch. Thicker is not stronger; over-thick resurfacer can shrink and crack. Fill deeper spalls and dips with a patcher or rapid-set repair mortar first, then apply the resurfacer at its recommended thickness across the slab.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to seal concrete resurfacer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is not required, but it is worthwhile outdoors in freeze-thaw climates. A penetrating silane or siloxane sealer reduces water and salt absorption, which protects the new surface from the same scaling that probably damaged the original slab. Let the resurfacer cure fully first, and check both the resurfacer and sealer labels for the minimum waiting period before sealing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use concrete resurfacer on a basement or garage floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Exterior resurfacers like Re-Cap work on garage floors and can refresh a worn basement slab. If you are laying tile, vinyl plank or carpet over an interior floor, an interior patch and skim coat such as Henry 549 FeatherFinish is the better choice because it trowels smoother and dries much faster. Never use interior-only products outdoors, where they will fail.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it cheaper to resurface or replace a concrete driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Resurfacing is almost always cheaper upfront, whether DIY or professional, because it avoids demolition, disposal and new concrete. It is only cheaper overall if the slab is structurally sound. Resurfacing a heaved, badly cracked or hollow-sounding driveway usually means paying for replacement a few years later anyway, so check the slab's condition before comparing quotes.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

